Title: THE WATER TANK — WORK DAY
Shoot: SAMPLE · project work day · 6 setups, shot in the order the work happens
Shoot-Type: C

[[SAMPLE CONTENT. Made up for Cari to judge the FORMAT. On a real work day the order is
whatever the work does — the tick list at the back is the thing to shoot against.]]

EXT. THE BARE PAD, EARLY — DAY

Jason lays the tools out on the tailgate where the camera can see them: a shovel, a rake, a four-foot level, a tamper, a tape, a bag of stakes and a ball of string.

!ON CAMERA: the tools laid out in a row, and Jason's hand touching each one as he names it.

JASON
(in his own words)
What the day is: a flat, hard circle, ten feet across, that will hold four tons of water and not move.

EXT. THE HIGH SIDE OF THE PAD — DAY

Jason drives a stake at the centre, ties the string to it and swings a five-foot circle, dragging a heel mark in the dirt as he goes.

!ON CAMERA: the string going tight, and the heel mark being dragged round into a full circle.

JASON
(in his own words)
Why the string: a circle you eyeball is an oval, and an oval puts the load on two edges.

EXT. INSIDE THE MARKED CIRCLE — DAY

Jason digs the high side down into the circle and throws the spoil outside the line. He never adds dirt to the low side.

!ON CAMERA: the shovel taking the high side down, and the growing pile of spoil OUTSIDE the mark.

JASON
(in his own words)
Dug ground holds. Filled ground settles. That is the whole rule, and it cost me a season to learn it.

EXT. THE HALF-DUG PAD — DAY

Jason lays the level across the hole in four directions like the spokes of a wheel, reading it each time and taking a little more off the high side.

!ON CAMERA: the bubble in the level, close enough to read on a phone screen.

JASON
(in his own words)
Four reads, not one. A pad can be level one way and a ramp the other.

EXT. THE LEVELLED PAD — DAY

Jason spreads crushed rock two inches deep and works it over with the rake, then runs the tamper across it in overlapping passes.

!ON CAMERA: the tamper landing, and the surface going from loose to tight under it.

JASON
(in his own words)
The rock is not the floor. The rock is what lets the water out from under the floor.

EXT. THE FINISHED PAD, TANK BEING WALKED ON — DAY

Cari and Jason walk the empty tank onto the pad on its side and stand it up. Jason walks round it once with a hand on the rim.

!ON CAMERA: the tank coming up onto its base, and the gap under the rim — or the lack of one — all the way round.

JASON
(in his own words)
If it rocks now, it rocks for the next ten years, so this is the last minute anybody can fix it for free.
